menu "LED Node Configuration"

    menu "Ring geometry"

        config LED_NODE_RING_LEDS
            int "Number of LEDs in the ring"
            default 60
            range 1 300
            help
                Physical pixel count. The spec assumes 60 (one per second on a
                clock face), which is what makes the countdown sweep land at
                exactly one pixel per 16.67 ms.

        config LED_NODE_DATA_GPIO
            int "Data GPIO"
            default 18
            help
                Drives the strip through a 74AHCT125 level shifter. 3.3V direct
                into a 5V strip is marginal and flaky.

        config LED_NODE_RING_OFFSET
            int "Pixel index at visual 12 o'clock"
            default 0
            range 0 299
            help
                The strip's seam can be mounted wherever is physically
                convenient; this rotates the logical ring to compensate.
                Animations work in degrees and never see a pixel index.

        config LED_NODE_RING_REVERSED
            bool "Reverse direction"
            default n
            help
                Enable if increasing pixel index runs counter-clockwise from
                the guest's point of view.

    endmenu

    menu "Command transport"

        choice LED_NODE_TRANSPORT
            prompt "Transport"
            default LED_NODE_TRANSPORT_HTTP
            help
                HTTP is the development transport: it leaves the USB port free
                for flashing, which the booth wiring otherwise occupies. UART is
                the booth transport. Both share one parser and one command
                vocabulary, so the swap is behavior-identical.

            config LED_NODE_TRANSPORT_HTTP
                bool "HTTP (development)"

            config LED_NODE_TRANSPORT_UART
                bool "UART (booth)"
        endchoice

        config LED_NODE_UART_PORT
            int "UART port number"
            depends on LED_NODE_TRANSPORT_UART
            default 0
            range 0 2
            help
                Port 0 is the one wired to the USB bridge, which is how the Pi
                connects. NOTE: that is also the console port. Set
                CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_NONE (or route the console to USB Serial JTAG
                on parts that have it) or ESP_LOG output will corrupt the
                protocol stream.

        config LED_NODE_UART_BAUD
            int "UART baud rate"
            depends on LED_NODE_TRANSPORT_UART
            default 115200

    endmenu

    config LED_NODE_DEFAULT_BRIGHTNESS
        int "Default global brightness (percent)"
        default 70
        range 1 100
        help
            Applied to every mode except Capture, which always runs at full
            brightness because PWM dimming bands the photo. Venue lighting
            varies, so this is runtime-settable too.

endmenu
